  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 4

## The move

Iteration 3 said the generator was exhausted before the domain was, and that
every candidate in three iterations came off one disassembly of one kernel
whose shape hides what allocation costs. This iteration changes the workload
instead of the compiler.

`kernel_wide.kotoba` is the same eight-round Lehmer step in eight independent
lanes, all live until a final sum. The x86-64 allocator hands out four
registers. Eight lanes do not fit in four registers.

## Measure

21 rotated samples per pair, every implementation answering 5224842816:

| workload | ISA | amu | rustc → LLVM | ratio |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| kernel (fits) | x86-64 | 14.9 ns | 12.4 ns | 1.20x |
| **kernel_wide** | **x86-64** | **85.1 ns** | **9.4 ns** | **9.07x** |
| **kernel_wide** | **AArch64** | **59.2 ns** | **6.5 ns** | **9.10x** |

On `kernel_wide`, amu's x86-64 output is 671 instructions: **221 stack loads,
166 stack stores, 80 push/pop — 70% memory traffic.** LLVM performs zero stack
loads for the same source, because the machine has sixteen general registers.

Both ISAs show the same factor. A cost that lands identically on two encoders
is not in either encoder.

## What this says about the day

Four peephole passes landed today took the narrow kernel from 896 bytes to 474
and its time from 1.81x to 1.20x. All four are correct and all four are
measured. **And the largest remaining difference is 9x, in a place none of them
touched, on a shape the only benchmark could not express.**

The corrected cost model from iteration 3 predicted this exactly: this core
charges for memory traffic. A four-register allocator on a workload needing more
than four produces memory traffic and nothing else does.

## Meta-review

- **The benchmark was the bottleneck, not the compiler.** Three iterations of
  candidate generation, ranking and refutation ran against a workload that
  could not express the dominant cost. Every conclusion drawn was locally
  correct and globally beside the point.
- **A ratio measured on one workload is a statement about that workload.**
  \"1.20x LLVM\" and \"9.07x LLVM\" are both true of the same compiler on the same
  day. Neither is the compiler's number; the compiler does not have one.
- **Ask what the benchmark cannot express before optimizing against it.** The
  cheap version of that question: what resource does this workload not exhaust?
  This one never needed a fifth register, and that was visible in its source
  from the beginning.
- The four peepholes stay. They are real and they compound with anything that
  follows; they were simply ranked against a number that had already stopped
  moving.

## Next

The allocator, and now it can be measured. Handing out more of the sixteen
registers x86-64 has, or eight of AArch64's thirty-one, should move a number
that is currently 9x — and unlike everything in iterations 1 through 3, there
is now a workload that will say whether it did."}]
